This article examines the existence of a positive weak solution to the following singular elliptic equation:
$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
-\Delta u &=& u^p\delta_{\Sigma} & \text{ in } \Omega, \\
u &=& 0 & \text{ on } \partial \Omega,
\end{array}
\right.
$$
where $\Omega$ is a bounded open subset of $\mathbb{R}^n$ ($n \geq 2$) with a smooth boundary, $\Sigma$ is a smooth hypersurface within $\Omega$, and $\delta_{\Sigma}$ represents the Dirac measure supported on that surface. Furthermore, assuming the nonlinearity takes the form $f(u) = u^p\delta_{\Sigma}$, we establish conditions under which a unique positive weak solution exists for the generalized problem $-\Delta u = f(u)\delta_{\Sigma}$ with $u=0$ on the boundary.
